## Commands Used

| Command                       | Purpose                                        |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `curl http://<host>:<port>`   | Tests connectivity from a client               |
| `ssh <user>@<host>`           | Connects to the remote server                  |
| `systemctl status <service>`  | Checks service status                          |
| `systemctl start <service>`   | Starts a service                               |
| `systemctl stop <service>`    | Stops a service                                |
| `systemctl disable <service>` | Prevents a service from starting automatically |
| `journalctl -xeu <service>`   | Displays detailed service logs                 |
| `netstat -tulpn`              | Shows listening ports and associated processes |
| `firewall-cmd`                | Manages firewalld rules                        |
| `iptables`                    | Manages Linux firewall rules                   |
| `service iptables save`       | Saves iptables configuration                   |

---

## Final Takeaway

Day 12 was a great example of how real-world DevOps troubleshooting works.

The problem was not solved by one command. It required following the evidence through multiple layers:

**Service → Port → Process → Firewall → Connectivity**

The biggest lesson I am taking from this exercise is:

> **Don't guess the root cause. Test each layer, follow the evidence, fix the problem, and verify the result from the user's perspective.**

This troubleshooting mindset will be useful when working with Linux servers, web applications, containers, cloud infrastructure, and production environments.
